From: Bedrock <bedrock@example.com>
Subject: [PATCH] x86/mm: Flush the TLB when clearing the Accessed bit

For deterministic execution under bedrock. x86 clears a PTE's (or PMD's)
Accessed bit in ptep_test_and_clear_young() / pmdp_test_and_clear_young()
without flushing the TLB; ptep_clear_flush_young() deliberately skips the
flush as a performance optimization. The CPU sets Accessed again only on a
page walk, i.e. on a TLB miss, so whether the bit is set at the next look
depends on whether the stale translation survived in the hardware TLB.
That is outside the guest's control and is not reproduced by a replay.

The kernel branches on the bit afterwards. For example, zap_pte_range()
tests pte_young() and calls folio_mark_accessed() while unmapping.

Flush the affected translation whenever the bit was actually cleared, so
the next access always walks the page tables and sets Accessed again.
Callers reached via /proc/<pid>/clear_refs, reclaim and MGLRU aging all
go through these two helpers, so this covers ptep_clear_flush_young() and
pmdp_clear_flush_young() as well.

Signed-off-by: Bedrock <bedrock@example.com>
---
 arch/x86/mm/pgtable.c | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/x86/mm/pgtable.c b/arch/x86/mm/pgtable.c
index 4a105f2..cee80b2 100644
--- a/arch/x86/mm/pgtable.c
+++ b/arch/x86/mm/pgtable.c
@@ -441,6 +441,9 @@ bool ptep_test_and_clear_young(struct vm_area_struct *vma,
 		ret = test_and_clear_bit(_PAGE_BIT_ACCESSED,
 					 (unsigned long *) &ptep->pte);
 
+	if (ret)
+		flush_tlb_page(vma, addr);
+
 	return ret;
 }
 
@@ -454,6 +457,9 @@ bool pmdp_test_and_clear_young(struct vm_area_struct *vma,
 		ret = test_and_clear_bit(_PAGE_BIT_ACCESSED,
 					 (unsigned long *)pmdp);
 
+	if (ret)
+		flush_tlb_range(vma, addr & PMD_MASK, (addr & PMD_MASK) + PMD_SIZE);
+
 	return ret;
 }
 #endif
-- 
